{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T03:21:29Z","input_tokens":1883,"cache_read_tokens":52480,"output_tokens":321,"content":"I've queued the per-author aggregation for approval.\n\n**What it does differently from the last run:** identical noise filters and identical formula — but it keeps the **author dimension** instead of collapsing to one line per day.\n\n```\ncontribution_level(day, author) = 0.5 × author_commits_that_day + 0.5 × log2(1 + author_real_lines_that_day)\n```\n\nSo each contributor gets their own series. Days a person didn't commit show `0`, which is what makes a multi-line chart readable.\n\n**Output:** top 8 contributors by total weighted contribution, plus the full 83-day matrix — one row per date, one column per author. It writes `/tmp/agor_contrib_by_author.json` and prints days 45–83 in compact form so I can build the complete chart without truncation issues.\n\n**Note on how this relates to the existing \"Contribution timeline\":** the per-author series will *not* sum exactly to the aggregate line you already have. That's mathematically expected — `log2` isn't additive across people, so the sum of per-author levels is not the same as the level of summed lines. The per-author chart is the right view for *\"who contributed how much\"*; the aggregate is the right view for *\"how much happened overall.\"* I'll mount it as a **separate multi-line widget** so both readings stay honest rather than overwriting the one you approved.\n\nApprove and I'll mount the multi-line per-contributor chart."}
